1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,078
This tutorial generates a tenancy contract
from a draft, and has

2
00:00:04,078 --> 00:00:07,785
the tenant review and accept it in the
tenant portal.

3
00:00:07,785 --> 00:00:10,380
As a Company Admin, open the draft

4
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:12,604
for unit A-201, Rajesh Kumar's contract.

5
00:00:12,604 --> 00:00:14,087
Its status is Draft.

6
00:00:14,087 --> 00:00:17,053
Open the Attachments tab and select
Preview Contract.

7
00:00:17,053 --> 00:00:21,130
The contract is rendered with the
organisation's details, the tenant, the

8
00:00:21,130 --> 00:00:25,208
term, every charge, and the cheques, and
nothing is saved yet.

9
00:00:25,208 --> 00:00:28,915
If anything is wrong, cancel and correct
the draft first.

10
00:00:28,915 --> 00:00:31,881
When it is right, select Confirm and Save.

11
00:00:31,881 --> 00:00:35,588
The contract is generated and numbered,
the status becomes Pending

12
00:00:35,588 --> 00:00:38,924
Signature, and the contract is listed
under Contract documents.

13
00:00:38,924 --> 00:00:41,149
Now sign in as the tenant.

14
00:00:41,149 --> 00:00:45,226
Rajesh's portal shows the new contract for
A-201, awaiting his signature.

15
00:00:45,226 --> 00:00:49,675
He can download it to read, and then
accept or reject it.

16
00:00:49,675 --> 00:00:51,899
Select Accept Tenancy Contract, and
confirm.

17
00:00:51,899 --> 00:00:56,348
The portal now says it was accepted, and
is awaiting the landlord.

18
00:00:56,348 --> 00:00:59,684
Had he rejected it, the contract would go
back

19
00:00:59,684 --> 00:01:02,650
to Draft, to be corrected and generated
again.

20
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:05,245
Back as the Company Admin, the contract

21
00:01:05,245 --> 00:01:07,840
shows Accepted by tenant, with the date.

22
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,288
It stays pending until it is posted, which
has its own tutorial.
